TEXT = """I am a blind man with a car. I know what that sounds like, and I know how blessed it is, because I know the other side of it. When I lost my sight, I lost the freedom to go where I wanted when I wanted, and I learned the difference between asking for a ride and being offered one. Asking costs something every time. Being offered costs nothing, and you remember it for years. So in our house a car is not quite a possession. It is the thing that lets Barb take Chris to his cardiologist and Tammy to her mother's bedside, and get Jimmy and me to church, and it is the thing we have been given, more than once, by people who did not have to.

## Grandpa's car

We moved into this house in November of the year I turned fifty. We brought a Chevy Impala with us, and as far as I was concerned, we were set for a while. It was in decent shape. I figured we would probably get another five or six years out of it. We parked it in the driveway because the garage was still full of the previous owner's things, and getting a different car was not something we needed to do.

Then my grandfather, Earl Clark, passed away, and his car became available to us. It was a Lincoln MKZ, a little older, but Grandpa had taken very good care of it.

That car was a lot nicer than anything we needed. Heated seats, all the bells and whistles, a very comfortable ride. I'm cold a lot, so heated seats are something I appreciate. There were practical limitations. The seats were low, and most of the people we gave rides to had some trouble getting in and out. We could comfortably take another couple, but that was about it. At the time it was mostly Barb and me, though, and it was a wonderful car for us. She drove it for about four years, and I was grateful we had it.

Grandpa's cane was in the trunk. It was a support cane, the kind you lean on, different from the white cane I carry. I wanted to keep it as something of his. There wasn't anything we needed to do with it. It was just Grandpa's cane, and it was there. I often thought of putting it inside the house, but I loved the idea of it being in the car with us.

## The shoulder

In October of 2024, Barb was driving on the west side of 465. There are four lanes where she was, and she was in the second lane from the right. Traffic was slowing in those right lanes as people were getting off at an exit, so she slowed with it. There was nobody behind her.

Then she looked in the rearview mirror and saw a truck coming up on her. He wasn't slowing down.

He hit the Lincoln square from behind and pushed the trunk up into the car. The damage was enough to total it, but what happened inside the car is the part that matters. Something in the driver's seat broke. The seat snapped back flat, and Barb went back with it. Her hands came off the wheel.

The car started drifting left. The traffic in the two lanes to her left was still moving at full speed, and she went across both of them without her hands on the wheel. At one point she could look up out the window and see cars flying past her.

She had already cleared those two lanes and reached the left shoulder before she was able to sit up some and grab the wheel. She got hold of it to keep the car from going back to the right, into the highway. Then she was able to get it under control and stopped.

I wasn't there to see any of that. My part began when the phone rang and she said, "Brian, I've been in an accident."

"Are you okay? Did you call 911?"

She hadn't. I asked if she thought she should, and she thought about it for a second and said she thought she should. I told her to call them and then call me back. While we were talking, I could hear the cars going by.

I was scared. She was on the phone with me, which was good, but she was still sitting out there, and I was trying to understand what had happened and what needed to happen next. When she called back, I started asking questions. Somewhere in that conversation I worked out that she was a long way from the truck, a quarter mile or so. She could see and hear the police back at the accident site, but nobody was coming up the shoulder to her.

So I told her to call 911 again. She ended up calling three times. She sat there for at least forty-five minutes before anybody came to check on her. When they finally did, they got her off the highway. I don't believe an ambulance came for her.

We felt right away that it was a miracle. I still do. Think about where she was: two lanes of traffic moving at highway speed, a broken seat, and no way to steer until she had already crossed those lanes. She got through without being hit again. I don't know what other word to use for that.

Later we went to get our belongings out of the Lincoln. We got what we could, but the trunk wouldn't open. Grandpa's cane was still in there, and we never got it back.

I'm grateful beyond anything that Barb got out of that car. I can be grateful for that and still be sad about the cane. It was something I had wanted to keep, and losing it wasn't a decision I got to make. When I think about the Lincoln now, that's one of the things I remember.

## The Patti Wagon

After that we needed another car. My mom, Patti, contributed a great deal of money toward it, and that made it possible for us to start looking.

The three of us who went to the used-car lot were quite a group. I had my white cane. Barb's mobility issues are apparent when you see her walking. Our neighbor Chris came with us, and although you wouldn't necessarily know it by looking at him, he is visually impaired too.

Chris and his wife Tammy live right across the street, and that is not an accident. We got to know them in my second year of blindness, which makes them lifelong friends in a way, because I became a new person in so many respects when I lost my sight, and they have known that person from the start. Part of the reason we bought this house was to live across from them. The Lord gave us two chances at it. We were going to buy the house next door to them until the one across the street came up.

The car we had gone there to see wasn't there, of course. So the salesmen started bringing other cars out for us to look at. I keep coming back to that detail. They were choosing which cars to bring out and show this particular group of people, and one of those cars was the one we ended up buying.

We took it for a test drive, with Barb driving and Chris and me along. We also went up the road to another lot and drove a Buick. We didn't go back to the first dealer that day. We went home, prayed about it, thought about it, and talked to people. We were trying to make a sensible decision with money that mattered a great deal to us.

The next day I called the first dealer. I asked whether he would take a thousand dollars off if I came in with a check, and he said that would be fine.

It was a 2017 Ford Flex with about sixty thousand miles on it. We asked for the CarFax, and he pulled it up. One owner. No accidents reported. It appeared to be in pretty good condition. We popped the hood and Barb looked in, since Chris and I couldn't. To be honest, I'm not sure that even when I had eyes I would have known what I was looking for under there. Being able to see something and knowing what it means are two different things.

After we had looked at the report, I asked the salesman directly. I asked whether the car had been in an accident. He indicated that it had not.

That was the information we had to work with. We had driven it, looked at the history, and asked the question. We trusted the answer. I wrote the check, and we took the car home. Being able to do that without taking on payments was a blessing in itself.

We called it the Patti Wagon because of how much my mom had put toward it. And it really was a great vehicle for what our lives were becoming.

It had a third row of seats that folded flat. When we later got Barb a mobility scooter, that turned out to be especially useful. Her scooter comes apart in pieces, and we could put the pieces in the back. If she needed it for a long walk, or to visit a consumer, or for any of the other things that are harder for her on foot, we had a way to bring it along. We hadn't even had the scooter when we bought the car, so we hadn't known how well that part would work out.

We also carried a lot of blind people in it over the next two years. We have always felt that part of our service is to look out for other blind people when they need a ride, and Chris and Tammy are first on that list. We have had a lot of trips and adventures together, in our vehicle or, when they had it, in their minivan, some memorable for the fun and some for the purpose. Neither Chris nor I can take a turn driving, and there are plenty of people we know who can't simply get in their own car and meet us somewhere. A vehicle with some extra room allows Barb to do more than get the two of us from one place to another.

This last year the purpose was the serious kind. Chris is a bypass brother of mine, and when he started feeling run down and fatigued, with pain in his left arm, we took him to his cardiologist. They found another small blockage, put in another stent, and he is back to feeling much better. More recently Tammy's mother was in the hospital, and Tammy needed ride after ride to take her turns at the bedside during her mother's last days. Both of those are thirty-minute drives, well past the distance an Uber makes sense for. That is the kind of thing a car with room in it is for.

On my birthday, May 1st, 2025, Jimmy came to live with us.

Jimmy had been a student of mine years earlier, when I taught technology at a blind rehab center. He had been living by himself in a place that wasn't being maintained and wasn't safe for him. There was another place he was supposed to move into, a room someone was going to rent to him, but the price kept changing and so did the date he could move in. Eventually we didn't trust that arrangement either.

We learned later that the woman who would have rented him the room died about five months after he was supposed to move there. She was on dialysis. She wouldn't have been able to look after him the way he needed. We didn't know that when we were trying to figure out what to do. At the time, we just knew the situation he was in wasn't good and the next one wasn't coming together.

So Jimmy came here. We had room in our house and room in our lives, and the car had room for him too. What had been a useful third row and some extra space became part of how a household of three worked.

I don't want to hurry past that as if Jimmy were one more practical detail in a story about a car. He is a blessing beyond measure. It is an honor and a privilege to help look after him. There is more to his story than belongs here, but he belongs in this one because the car served his life along with ours. When I talk about what we needed, or what God has provided for us, I am talking about Jimmy too.

Barb also made several trips to Tennessee in the Patti Wagon to spend a week with Keshie. Keshie is something like a daughter to us, someone else who has become part of our family. Both her parents are gone. She uses a wheelchair, and a few years ago she moved to Tennessee to get married. Maintaining her independence and getting through everyday life without much support has been a struggle.

Barb would go down to help look after her and give her husband, Evan, a break. Those visits took a dependable car and enough room to bring what Barb needed. They were another reason we were grateful for the Flex. It let her be there in person for someone who needed her.

And on May 22nd of this year, Barb's birthday, a Friday, it took us to Wisconsin. Our son AJ called with an emergency, the kind you cannot handle over the phone, though we tried all morning. Barb was already worn out. We did not get on the road until about noon. One thing I will say about that week, because it is His doing and not mine: after nearly six months without a single student, two had come to me in March, and the first pay in two months had landed that May, in time. I had not planned it. It was simply there, a week before we needed it. We drove up, did what had to be done, barely got to see him, and turned around. It rained on the last part of the drive, and Barb hates driving in the rain; her glasses do not do well with the glare. We got home at one in the morning. I tried hard to stay awake for Barb and could not. She told me afterward that from Lebanon on, a good forty-five minutes from our house, she does not remember how we got home.

I remember waking somewhere on that road with a feeling I have never had before or since. God was getting us home. He was pleased with what we had done that day, and He was giving us exactly enough strength to finish it. Since that night I have felt that if I keep my focus on Him and do what He wants, it is going to work out, and I do not have to carry it. That feeling has gotten me through some hard weeks since. I know now that He was holding that car not only together, but on the road too.

The Patti Wagon's last big trip was in early August. My mom, Barb, and I went to Gatlinburg, Tennessee, for a reunion of my mom's family. It was the first time everyone had been together since my stepfather Marty passed away, and it was my first real vacation in years.

We stayed in condos on the edge of town. Apparently the view was amazing. That's what the sighted people told me, and ChatGPT agreed with them. There were bears around too. One walked right past our window, so I expect the Patti Wagon got a closer look at a bear than it was used to.

That was a good trip for the three of us. The car got us there and back, and we had time with family that we hadn't had in a long while. I include it because the car wasn't only useful when there was an emergency or somebody needed looking after. We got to enjoy ourselves in it too.

About a month before the trip, Barb had replaced the electronic key while she was visiting Keshie. Then, while we were in Tennessee, my uncle Rob noticed the tires needed replacing, so we put new ones on. We had been spending money to keep it going, with every expectation that we would keep using it.

## Saturday, October 3rd

On Saturday, October 3rd, we stopped at a student's house and then went out to my mom's for dinner. As Barb was turning around in the driveway, we felt the transmission slip.

We went inside and ate, but when we came back out and tried to leave, it was obvious something was wrong. Every time the car changed gears, you could hear and feel a clunk. It was barely drivable. We put it back in Mom's driveway, and she drove us home. The next day we had it towed to Cangany Automotive, where we have taken our cars for years.

Mike, the manager, explained what they had found. While testing the transmission, they noticed that the radiator wasn't mounted properly. As they looked closer, they could tell from the welds that the car had been in a front-end collision and hadn't been repaired correctly. The hood had been replaced too. Mike explained that the improperly mounted radiator had led to the transmission trouble.

He ran his own CarFax. It said the same thing ours had said two years earlier: no accident reported.

That was hard to hear. I had stood there at the dealership and asked the very question that should have mattered. I had explained that I couldn't see. I wasn't expecting someone to guarantee that an older car would never break down; I wanted to know whether it had been in an accident. The answer I was given was no, and now our mechanic was telling us it had been wrecked and improperly repaired.

Knowing that didn't give us a working car, though. We still had to decide what we could reasonably do with the one we had.

A replacement transmission would cost eleven thousand dollars. Mike knew the model and said there was also a good chance we would be looking at a water pump problem before too long, another four or five thousand dollars. We were talking about a nine-year-old vehicle with just under a hundred thousand miles on it. If I was going to put fifteen thousand dollars or more into a car, I wanted to have some confidence that we would get a great deal more use out of it. I couldn't see how to justify that expense with everything else we now knew.

We decided not to fix it.

We had paid seventeen thousand dollars for the Patti Wagon and gotten two years and a little over thirty thousand miles out of it. That's a lot of money for two years. I don't need to pretend otherwise to be grateful for the car. I can be unhappy about what we were told when we bought it and still look at those thirty thousand miles and see how much God allowed us to do with them.

It got Barb to Keshie. It took us to Wisconsin when AJ needed us. It carried Jimmy and the scooter and other people who needed rides. It took us on a vacation. All that time, there was damage we knew nothing about, and the car kept going. Once Mike explained what had happened, I thought back over those trips differently. God had held that car together for us while we were using it.

Now we had to work out what came next, with considerably less money available than when we bought it.

## The wait

I need to explain a little about work and money, because otherwise it might sound as if we simply chose to wait a few weeks before buying another car.

The business I work for, First Aid for the Blind, gets its students through Vocational Rehabilitation. VR is the state agency that helps people retrain when they lose their sight, and its funding comes through the federal government and the Department of Education. With the changes in Washington, the referrals dried up.

This wasn't only happening to us. Nearly every blind organization we talked with was experiencing some version of the same thing. People still needed services, but the process that got them to us had stopped working the way it had before. I went close to six months without a student.

Two students came in March. The pay that arrived in May, in time for that trip to Wisconsin, came from that work. Then there was another dry stretch until summer. One of the students I worked with then was deaf-blind. That's unusual for me, and whenever I have the opportunity to teach someone who is deaf-blind, I find myself facing my own worst fear. I depend so much on my hearing. Working with that person makes me grateful all over again that I have it.

Another student was a woman losing the little sight she had left while she was caring for someone who could barely get around. There were parts of her life I understood without needing much explanation.

The work matters to me for reasons that go beyond the income. I know there are people who need what I can teach them. I know something about the adjustments they are facing and about what becomes possible when they have the right tools. So a long stretch with no students leaves me with more than a financial question. I start wondering what God wants me to be doing if the people aren't coming through the routes I've been accustomed to.

At the same time, the expenses of living keep coming. Over the past couple of years we had dealt with the air conditioning, the furnace, a fallen tree, the water softener, plumbing problems, and Barb's teeth. A string of those things takes money out of savings, and a lean year makes it harder to put it back.

But I want to be very clear about what our life is like. Our needs are met. There are three disabled people in this house, and we live far better than most blind people I know. We've become more disciplined about money, and we do not suffer one bit.

Barb gets to take a vacation from time to time. Jimmy can give up some of the things he wants so he can help people in Nigeria who have much less than he does. I get to work with AI and do things I never imagined being able to do when I could see. We have two amazing dogs, both appropriately lazy, and I never have to mow the yard. There is usually Cookies and Cream in the freezer, which Jimmy and I consider a fairly important part of the arrangement.

God provides for us in abundance. What we have shapes what we want, and I'm fine with that. There is a big difference between having to think carefully about a purchase and having a miserable life. We have a very good life.

My dad has made it possible for us to have no house payment, which is an enormous gift. I don't want to add a car payment when I can't tell you how much work I'll have next month, much less for the next several years. That's why, when the Flex quit, I wasn't comfortable going out and financing its replacement. We needed to look at the money we actually had and figure out what was possible.

A year ago, I think I would have turned that into a project that occupied every spare minute. I would have researched makes and models, compared prices, looked through dealer inventory, called people, and arranged to go see cars. I would have felt I was doing my part by keeping at it until I found something.

This time we got a general idea of what was available and what our money could buy, but I didn't keep pursuing particular cars. We waited and prayed. I had other work in front of me, and I felt very strongly that I needed to keep doing it.

For about a month, much of my spare time had been going into blindnessinthebible.com. I started studying the places where the Bible talks about blindness, both physical blindness and spiritual blindness, and the project kept growing. The more I looked, the more there was to consider.

Jesus healed the blind as no one before Him had, and no one since. Healing the blind was part of the prophecy of who He was. As a blind man, I find comfort in spending time with that. Blindness is something I've lived with and taught people about for years, and here I was looking at it again through Scripture and finding enough to keep me occupied far longer than I expected.

The website has grown into something much bigger than I set out to make. There are now YouTube episodes about twenty minutes long, with music and voices. People have responded in ways I wouldn't have predicted. I've been able to make things and share things that, a few years ago, I wouldn't have known how to begin.

That fits with work I've always felt called to do: helping sighted people understand blind people and raising their expectations of us. When a sighted person meets me, there are usually already ideas about what I can do, what I can't do, what I must be afraid of, and what help I need. Often those ideas come from people who mean well. They can still limit me.

It takes time to change those expectations. I want people to have enough information and enough contact with our lives that they can start to reconsider them. And if something I say changes what a person expects from the next blind person they meet, the benefit reaches farther than me. That's part of what makes the website worth the time it takes.

Then there is Nigeria. Around the first of September, our church, Hope Christian Church, was thinking about new ministries. I began considering what a small church could do, what Jimmy and I knew about blindness, and what might happen if we put those things together. I started studying blindness in Nigeria.

Seventy percent of blind Nigerians have to beg. Roughly half of the people with visual impairment there could be treated, often with a surgery that costs about a hundred US dollars. We are making connections now with blind homes outside Lagos.

When you start looking at what a relatively small amount of money might do there, it's hard to put that information aside and go back to thinking only about your own circumstances. A small church doesn't have to wait until it becomes a large church to consider helping. There is work to do in understanding the needs, finding people to work with, and making sure that help can get where it is supposed to go.

That's the stage I'm in. There is research to do and groundwork to lay. I need to make connections and think through how things can work safely before asking people to fund something larger. I feel like I'm helping put together something far bigger than me, but the next piece of it still has to be done, and right now I can do that piece.

Around the same time, I finished a music album, A Series of Joyful Noises. That has a story of its own, and I'll leave it for another day. But once my attention turned toward the music and Nigeria, the teaching work started coming again.

Seven new referrals in eight weeks, more than the rest of the year combined. I have just finished working with the second student, and the third is lined up. He is a blind pastor, of all things.

The timing has given me something I needed. I can pay for the little things involved in getting the Nigeria work started without having to stop and look for funding right now. Eventually that will have to come, and I believe God will provide it. For now the teaching work allows me to keep going with what's in front of me.

That's where my attention was while we were without a car. I was busy, and I felt I was being given work to do. I didn't want the car search to take all the time and energy that could go into it.

There was also a practical fact I kept reminding myself of: I don't have to have a car to teach over Zoom. I don't need a car to work on a website. The work in Nigeria is a world away whether there's a vehicle in our garage or not. Losing our transportation complicated our lives, but it didn't make those things impossible.

We live in Indianapolis, where we can have groceries and all kinds of other things delivered. People from church took us to church and Bible study. They were generous with their time and their willingness to help, and that made a real difference. We were getting through the weeks.

Of course a car would help. It would help Barb do her job. It would help with errands and the ordinary things three people need to do. I didn't have to convince myself we would never need one in order to keep working while we waited. We could acknowledge the need, be grateful for the help we were receiving, and carry on with the things we were still able to do.

That's what we were trying to do. Some days I felt better about it than others.

## Tuesday

On Tuesday, October 20th, my mom came over to check on me. We usually see her about once a week, but without a car it had been harder to get out to her house. I had also been teaching all day for the previous two weeks, and I hadn't been doing much texting or catching up with people.

By the time the students were finished, there were things to do at home. Barb would get in from work and I would help her get settled so she could get a good night's rest. Jimmy needed to eat. By evening I was tired. There wasn't anything mysterious about why Mom hadn't heard much from me, but she came over, and I'm glad she did.

We talked for about an hour. I expressed some frustration, and I don't think there was anything unreasonable about it or that it came out in a particularly negative way. I felt we were doing God's work. I know He tests our faith sometimes, and I'm not one to question that, but I also felt my faith was already pretty strong. This seemed like a difficult time for another test.

That was more or less where I was. I wasn't giving up on what I believed I was supposed to do. I was tired, and I had questions about how we were going to manage and whether I had my attention in the right place. Keeping the website and Nigeria in front of me made sense, but our household still had needs. Those thoughts were there together, and talking to Mom gave me room to say them.

What she gave me that afternoon was reassurance that my heart and my focus were in the right place. That mattered more than anything else she could have said at that moment. I had been wondering whether I was doing the right thing, and she helped steady me.

I want to give that conversation its place in the story, because it's easy to hurry on to the car. Before any car appeared, my mom had already helped me. She had come over, listened, and told me she believed I was focused where I should be. I needed that, and I received it from her.

When she went home that evening, she put a short post on the Facebook page for her neighborhood, a retirement community on the northeast side. I never saw the post. From what I understand, it simply said that her son was blind and we were looking for a car. She didn't tell the whole story or make a big appeal.

Within two hours, Betty called her.

## Betty and Bob

Betty and Bob live a few houses down from my mom. They are both eighty-five, have been married sixty-five years, and have known each other since the fifth grade. They grew up in a suburb of Chicago. By high school there was a romantic interest, and all these years later they're still together. They have two children.

Their daughter had bought a new car and given them her previous one, another Hyundai. That left them with two vehicles and no particular reason to keep both. The one they were willing to sell was a 2011 Hyundai Veracruz with 220,000 miles on it.

That's an older car with a lot of miles. I knew that going in. But there was quite a bit more to know about it, beginning with the fact that Betty and Bob liked it so much they were still driving it while the newer vehicle sat in the garage.

Betty didn't want to advertise it online. She didn't want to have a lot of strangers calling about the car. Then she saw Mom's post, and here was somebody looking for exactly what they had to offer, without her having to list it anywhere. She told us later how glad she was not to have to deal with all those calls.

Mom told her more about us when they talked that night, including the work I was doing. By the time I met Betty, she already knew about the website.

The next day, Wednesday, Mom took her own car over to their house, left it in their driveway, and brought the Veracruz to ours. We could look it over, take it for a drive, and make sure it would fit in the garage. The Flex had only cleared the door by a couple of inches, so that was something we wanted to check. The Veracruz is shorter, and it fit.

I know what I can learn about a car by putting my hands on it. I can feel the condition of the interior, whether things are loose or coming apart, and whether the places people use every day have been taken care of. This one felt like a new car to me. Everything was where it belonged. There was one little metal piece by the passenger door handle that had come loose, with a scrap of cloth over it. Apart from that, I wasn't finding things falling off or hanging loose.

Then there were the service records. The stack was about as thick as my hand, and they had kept track of everything: oil changes, tire rotations, light bulbs, windshield wipers. It was the kind of record that tells you how someone has approached owning a car over a long time.

At about 190,000 miles, the transmission had needed replacing. They had gone ahead and replaced it. They liked the vehicle enough, and had enough confidence in it, to keep it going. Everything about how they had cared for it suggested they hadn't expected to need another one.

We took it around the block. We had loved the way the Patti Wagon felt, particularly its size and height. After what happened to Barb in the Lincoln, the feeling of being in a bigger vehicle matters to us. The Veracruz felt even better. It felt heavier and had more pickup, and it was comfortable.

And then we kept finding things we liked. Heated seats, which you already know will get my attention. Separate heat and air controls for the different parts of the car, including the back. An armrest for Jimmy and a place for his drink. The third row was more comfortable than the Flex's. There was a rubber mat for the back when the seats were folded down, and even that felt as though it had barely been used.

We had been looking for a way to get three people to church and back and to manage the rest of our lives. We weren't shopping for all these extras. But there they were, and I enjoyed discovering them. It was more than enough for what we needed, in ways that were going to make everyday use of it pleasant for all three of us.

They were asking $3,900. When my mom went to write the check, Betty told her to make it $3,000.

I don't want to pass over that either. Nine hundred dollars is a lot of money to us. They were already making the car available at a price we could manage, and then she lowered it when it was time to pay. That was another blessing.

When we walked into their house, Betty took hold of me, hugged me, and kissed me on the cheek. I'm not much of a hugger. People who know me will tell you I'm not generally the cuddly, touchy-feely person in the room. I'm getting better about it. That particular greeting was wonderful.

We stayed and talked for about an hour. I spent some of my growing-up years in Downers Grove, so we had Chicago in common. We talked about places we remembered and things we missed. They told us about their family, and Betty told us she still keeps up with a group of about ten people who went to Catholic grade school and middle school with her. Those friendships have lasted sixty-some years.

It was good to sit and listen to them. We had come because of a car, and we got to spend time with two people whose lives had touched ours very generously. The conversation gave us a chance to know a little more about them than a price, a mileage figure, and a set of service records. We had things to talk about, and we enjoyed the fellowship.

We drove the Veracruz home on Wednesday, October 21st. Its first real trip was to Bible study the next morning.

## What I make of it

There are things we could have done differently. We probably should have taken it over to Mike and had him look at it before we handed over a check. We didn't. I know that, and I'm not trying to turn every decision we made into an example of how everybody ought to buy a car.

I can't tell you how many miles we will get out of it. If it falls apart tomorrow, that won't change what this has meant to me. God provided for us through these people, at this time, and I believe He will provide again when we need Him to. That's how I'm able to receive this car without needing to know its whole future first.

We haven't settled on a name yet. Its plate says "In God We Trust," and the color is a dark brown, like an old penny, so Penny is one possibility. BB is another, with Betty and Bob, Barb and Brian all contributing Bs. We'll see what sticks.

When I look back over the whole thing, there are a lot of people in it. My dad. My mom. Grandpa. The people who gave us rides while we waited. Betty and Bob. There are students who came when the work was needed, and there is work I can do now that I didn't imagine doing a few years ago. There is a household to care for and a much larger need in Nigeria that I am only beginning to understand how to help with.

I don't know how to separate the car from all of that, and I don't think I need to. It is part of the way we are being cared for while we do the things in front of us. Some of those things are as ordinary as getting Jimmy fed and helping Barb get settled after work. Some reach much farther away. They all belong to the life God has given us.

That's what I want people to hear when I tell this. I didn't arrange for the pay to arrive when AJ needed us. I didn't make seven referrals arrive after months of almost no work. I didn't know there were two people a few houses down from my mom who had an extra car they loved and didn't want to advertise. I was doing what I could with what I understood, and other people were doing what they could too. Looking back, I can see His provision in far more than the final answer to the car problem.

Grandpa's cane is still in a trunk somewhere. I have stopped minding as much. The One who crossed two lanes of 465 with no hands on the wheel, and brought us home from Wisconsin asleep, and held a wrecked car together for thirty thousand miles, knows where it is.

Grandpa doesn't need his cane anymore. I can thank Jesus for that. I can thank Him, too, because He provides for my every need, and because of Him I know I'll see Grandpa again someday.
"""


def sections() -> list[tuple[str, list[str]]]:
    """(heading, paragraphs) in order; the first heading is empty for the opening."""
    out: list[tuple[str, list[str]]] = [("", [])]
    for para in TEXT.strip().split("\n\n"):
        para = para.strip()
        if para.startswith("## "):
            out.append((para[3:].strip(), []))
        elif para:
            out[-1][1].append(" ".join(para.split()))
    return out
